Q: Unit test separation A: <p>Your intuition is correct - splitting into multiple tests is the better practice. In unit testing, we generally follow the One-Test-One-Assertion (OTOA) principle (<a href="https://treatwell.engineering/the-importance-of-one-test-one-assertion-otoa-in-unit-testing-8ff68c6445bf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">more here</a>) and aim that our tests have to be independent from each other. The main reasons:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Isolation of failures. If your first assertion in <code>interpolateTest</code> fails, JUnit stops there - you have no idea whether the remaining assertions pass or fail. One broken behaviour hides everything after it. Suppose later you refactor <code>StringFormatter.interpolate</code> to throw a custom <code>StringFormattingException</code> instead of <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>. In your version, all you see is &quot;interpolateTest failed&quot; — you have to read the stack trace to find out which of seven assertions broke</p>
</li>
<li><p>Independent test can have independent setup per scenario, which matters a lot when your test mutates state (not like your static <code>setInterpolateFailsOnNull</code> flag, because it changes static state, but if you have non-static state for your object under the test)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>These are only 2 reasons came to my mind, please google others, I think you will find some.</p>
<p>So, in your case I would split for example as follows:</p>
<p>First test:</p>
<pre><code>// Sunny day scenario 1
// here we expect result in String format back
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource(&quot;successfulInterpolationCases&quot;)
void interpolateReturnsExpectedString(String expected, Object[] args) {
    String input = &quot;Hi, I am {} and it is {}℃ out&quot;;
    assertEquals(expected, StringFormatter.interpolate(input, args));
}

static Stream&lt;Arguments&gt; successfulInterpolationCases() {
    return Stream.of(
        Arguments.of(&quot;Hi, I am Andrew and it is 25℃ out&quot;, new Object[]{&quot;Andrew&quot;, 25}),
        Arguments.of(&quot;Hi, I am Andrew and it is 25℃ out&quot;, new Object[]{&quot;Andrew&quot;, 25, &quot;extra arg&quot;}),
        Arguments.of(&quot;Hi, I am Andrew and it is {}℃ out&quot;, new Object[]{&quot;Andrew&quot;}),
        Arguments.of(&quot;Hi, I am {} and it is {}℃ out&quot;,     new Object[]{})
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Second test:</p>
<pre><code>// Sunny day scenario 2
// We do some additional StringFormatter setup here, so we keep this test separate from the first sunny-day test cases.

// We still need to call StringFormatter.setInterpolateFailsOnNull(true) at the end of the test, 
// because I assume it modifies static state. Using static state for this is generally not a good architectural decision, 
// because once the state is changed, it remains that way until it is explicitly reset, which can affect other tests. 
// I would recommend creating a StringFormatter instance using the `new` keyword and making 
// setInterpolateFailsOnNull an instance (non-static) method that operates on instance fields instead.
@Test
void givenFormatterNotFailsOnNullWhenInterpolateThenReturnsExpectedString() {
        try {
            StringFormatter.setInterpolateFailsOnNull(false);

            assertEquals(&quot;Hi, I am null and it is 25℃ out&quot;, StringFormatter.interpolate(input, null, 25));

        } finally {
            StringFormatter.setInterpolateFailsOnNull(true);
        }      
}
</code></pre>
<p>Third test:</p>
<pre><code>// Rainy day scenario
@Test
void interpolateThrowsExceptionOnNullArgument() {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -&gt; StringFormatter.interpolate(input, null, &quot;25&quot;));
}
</code></pre>

Q: C# WPF Enlarge userControl till the ScrollViewer inside won&#39;t show its scrollbar A: <p>I assume you have this layout</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;UserControl x:Class=&quot;SomeNamespace.SomeUserControl&quot;
             ------------------------
             MouseDoubleClick=&quot;userControl_MouseDoubleClick&quot;&gt;

    &lt;ScrollViewer Name=&quot;scrollcontainer&quot; MinWidth=&quot;24&quot; MinHeight=&quot;24&quot; Margin=&quot;4,32,4,16&quot; VerticalScrollBarVisibility=&quot;Auto&quot;
                  Padding=&quot;4&quot; ScrollChanged=&quot;scrollcontainer_ScrollChanged&quot;&gt;
        &lt;!-- Some content --&gt;
    &lt;/ScrollViewer&gt;

&lt;/UserControl&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The required actions (if I understand correctly):</p>
<ul>
<li>in its initial state, the ScrollViewer's contents are partially displayed and scrollable.</li>
<li>when double-clicked, the contents should expand to full size, similar to if the ScrollViewer were not present.</li>
<li>when clicked again, it should return to its original state.</li>
</ul>
<p>My proposed solution.
Layout:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;UserControl x:Class=&quot;SomeNamespace.SomeUserControl&quot;
             ----------------------
             MouseDoubleClick=&quot;userControl_MouseDoubleClick&quot;&gt;

    &lt;!-- Main ContentControl handles external margins, sizes, and explicit border thickness --&gt;
    &lt;ContentControl x:Name=&quot;mainControl&quot;
                    Margin=&quot;4,32,4,16&quot; 
                    Padding=&quot;4&quot;
                    BorderThickness=&quot;1&quot;
                    BorderBrush=&quot;LightGray&quot;&gt;  &lt;!-- Set the required size (Thickness) and color here --&gt;

        &lt;!-- ScrollViewer is now borderless and has no extra margins --&gt;
        &lt;ScrollViewer Name=&quot;scrollcontainer&quot;
                      MinWidth=&quot;24&quot; 
                      MinHeight=&quot;24&quot; 
                      Margin=&quot;0&quot; 
                      Padding=&quot;0&quot; 
                      BorderThickness=&quot;0&quot;
                      Background=&quot;Transparent&quot;
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility=&quot;Auto&quot;
                      ScrollChanged=&quot;scrollcontainer_ScrollChanged&quot;&gt;

            &lt;!-- Some content --&gt;

        &lt;/ScrollViewer&gt;
    &lt;/ContentControl&gt;

&lt;/UserControl&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Handler code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>private ScrollViewer _scrollContainerField;

private void userControl_MouseDoubleClick(object sender, MouseButtonEventArgs e)
{
    if (_scrollContainerField == null)
    {
        _scrollContainerField = scrollcontainer;
        object content = _scrollContainerField.Content;

        _scrollContainerField.Content = null;
        mainControl.Content = content;
    }
    else
    {
        object content = mainControl.Content;

        mainControl.Content = null;
        _scrollContainerField.Content = content;
        mainControl.Content = _scrollContainerField;

        _scrollContainerField = null;
    }

    e.Handled = true;
}
</code></pre>
